      <main id="content"><h1>FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle: mathematical reading route</h1><p>Read the statements and derivations in source order. Every result has its own optional Lean statement and proof. Source assumptions, library reuse and unproved boundaries are kept explicit.</p><ol><li><a href="#decl-autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgauss-466257d4a8">Actual full-range proximal Gaussian oracle</a></li></ol><section id="decl-autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgauss-466257d4a8"><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual full-range proximal Gaussian oracle</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</code> · theorem · <a href="index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h3>Mathematical statement</h3><p class="reader-claim">Produce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the genuine quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and fixed-eta nonexpansivity. The literal Gaussian gradient evaluator G(s,z)=gradientV(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z) is jointly measurable and obeys input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta for equal eta. Produce an actual Markov kernel K(s)=stdGaussian.map(G(s,-)). This covers the exact evaluator (3.2) and only Lemma4.2 (4.4)-(4.5), with no posterior or finite-query claim.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[F_s(x)=V(x)+\|x-y_s\|^2/(2\eta_s),\quad p_s+\eta_s\nabla V(p_s)=y_s,\quad G(s,z)=\nabla V(p_s+\sqrt{\eta_s}z),\quad K_s=G(s,\cdot)_\#\gamma.\]</div><h4>Proof roadmap</h4><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Derive the real gradient estimates</strong><p>Reuse the actual C2 Hessian-to-gradient and strong-convexity APIs. The source upper bound supplies gradientV Lipschitz1. The convex gradient-step theorem supplies id-gradientV Lipschitz1, and convexity supplies monotonicity. None is a theorem premise.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Construct a uniformly damped map</strong><p>Use a real sufficient implementation of the source-implicit proximal existence step. For each actual parameter, damp the equation by (1+eta)^-1. Its contraction factor is eta/(1+eta)&lt;=1/2 throughout 0&lt;eta&lt;=1, including eta1. This is a mathematical construction, not a numerical query-cost assertion.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates</strong><p>Start every iteration at zero. Actual measurable eta/y and the continuous id-gradient map make every parameterized iterate measurable. Banach convergence and measurable limit prove measurability of the actual selector, rather than assuming a measurable choice.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Recover the exact source equation</strong><p>Multiply the actual fixed-point equation by positive 1+eta and cancel the scalar inverse. The resulting equality is the literal proximal first-order condition for the source quadratic potential.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness</strong><p>Differentiate the true quadratic regularization. Its gradient vanishes at the constructed p. Existing strong-convexity and first-order bounds give the exact coefficient (kappa^-1+eta^-1)/2. Positivity forces norm(z-p)^2=0 for any competing lower point.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta</strong><p>Subtract the two literal proximal equations only when eta_s=eta_t. Monotonicity bounds the inner product below by the squared proximal distance. Cauchy-Schwarz and positive-norm cancellation give nonexpansivity; a zero-distance branch is handled separately.</p></li><li><span>7</span><strong>Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator</strong><p>The actual measurable p, eta square root and noise coordinate give a jointly measurable input to the continuous gradient. No posterior identification or oracle witness is used.</p></li><li><span>8</span><strong>Prove the exact input and noise constants</strong><p>Apply the true gradient Lipschitz bound, split the input difference with the triangle inequality and use proximal nonexpansivity at equal eta. The literal square-root coefficient stays outside the noise norm. This supplies both source inequalities (4.4)-(4.5).</p></li><li><span>9</span><strong>Realize the actual Markov kernel</strong><p>Take the product of the identity kernel and the constant standard Gaussian kernel, then map it by G. Joint measurability proves it Markov; dirac-product and map-map give the exact pointwise pushforward. This gradient-output law is distinct from the RGO posterior and HMC transitions.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h4>All objects and hypotheses</h4><ul><li>Source beta=1, alpha=kappa^-1, real kappa&gt;=1; actual C2 V with kappa^-1*normv^2 &lt;= D2V(x)[v,v] &lt;= normv^2 for every x,v.</li><li>Finite real complete Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; arbitrary measurable parameter space S; actual measurable eta:S-&gt;R and y:S-&gt;E with 0&lt;eta(s)&lt;=1 for every s. These coordinate-free/parameter-space extensions are disclosed.</li><li>No supplied proximal point, minimizer, measurable selector, gradient estimate, Lipschitz bound or Markov kernel. All are produced from the literal potential and actual Gaussian pushforward. The source numerical-prox call cap eta&lt;=1/(2beta) remains a separate computational contract.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h3>Mathematical proof</h3><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>1. Derive the real gradient estimates</h5><p>Reuse the actual C2 Hessian-to-gradient and strong-convexity APIs. The source upper bound supplies gradientV Lipschitz1. The convex gradient-step theorem supplies id-gradientV Lipschitz1, and convexity supplies monotonicity. None is a theorem premise.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Lip}(\nabla V)\le1,\quad\operatorname{Lip}(I-\nabla V)\le1,\quad\langle\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(z),x-z\rangle\ge0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>actual_gradient_bounds; QuadraticRegularization; SmoothnessEquivalences; GradientDescentContraction; StrongConvexFirstOrder</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>2. Construct a uniformly damped map</h5><p>Use a real sufficient implementation of the source-implicit proximal existence step. For each actual parameter, damp the equation by (1+eta)^-1. Its contraction factor is eta/(1+eta)&lt;=1/2 throughout 0&lt;eta&lt;=1, including eta1. This is a mathematical construction, not a numerical query-cost assertion.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[T_s(x)=(1+\eta_s)^{-1}[y_s+\eta_s(x-\nabla V(x))],\quad \operatorname{Lip}(T_s)\le\eta_s/(1+\eta_s)\le1/2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>parameterized_damped_point; ContractingWith</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>3. Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates</h5><p>Start every iteration at zero. Actual measurable eta/y and the continuous id-gradient map make every parameterized iterate measurable. Banach convergence and measurable limit prove measurability of the actual selector, rather than assuming a measurable choice.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[p_s=\lim_{n\to\infty}T_s^n(0),\quad s\mapsto p_s\text{ measurable}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContractingWith.fixedPoint; tendsto_iterate_fixedPoint; measurable_of_tendsto_metrizable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>4. Recover the exact source equation</h5><p>Multiply the actual fixed-point equation by positive 1+eta and cancel the scalar inverse. The resulting equality is the literal proximal first-order condition for the source quadratic potential.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[p_s+\eta_s\nabla V(p_s)=y_s.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContractingWith.fixedPoint_isFixedPt; smul_smul; mul_inv_cancel₀</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>5. Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness</h5><p>Differentiate the true quadratic regularization. Its gradient vanishes at the constructed p. Existing strong-convexity and first-order bounds give the exact coefficient (kappa^-1+eta^-1)/2. Positivity forces norm(z-p)^2=0 for any competing lower point.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[F_s(p_s)+\tfrac12(\kappa^{-1}+\eta_s^{-1})\|z-p_s\|^2\le F_s(z),\quad F_s(z)\le F_s(p_s)\iff z=p_s.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>actual_quadratic_minimum; HasFDerivAt.norm_sq; QuadraticRegularization; StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>6. Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta</h5><p>Subtract the two literal proximal equations only when eta_s=eta_t. Monotonicity bounds the inner product below by the squared proximal distance. Cauchy-Schwarz and positive-norm cancellation give nonexpansivity; a zero-distance branch is handled separately.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\eta_s=\eta_t\Longrightarrow\|p_s-p_t\|^2\le\langle y_s-y_t,p_s-p_t\rangle\Longrightarrow\|p_s-p_t\|\le\|y_s-y_t\|.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn; real_inner_le_norm; norm_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>7. Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator</h5><p>The actual measurable p, eta square root and noise coordinate give a jointly measurable input to the continuous gradient. No posterior identification or oracle witness is used.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[G(s,z)=\nabla V(p_s+\sqrt{\eta_s}z),\quad G\text{ measurable on }S\times E.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measurable.comp; Measurable.sqrt; Measurable.smul; LipschitzWith.continuous</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>8. Prove the exact input and noise constants</h5><p>Apply the true gradient Lipschitz bound, split the input difference with the triangle inequality and use proximal nonexpansivity at equal eta. The literal square-root coefficient stays outside the noise norm. This supplies both source inequalities (4.4)-(4.5).</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\eta_s=\eta_t\Longrightarrow\|G(s,z)-G(t,w)\|\le\|y_s-y_t\|+\sqrt{\eta_s}\|z-w\|.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul; norm_add_le; norm_smul; Real.sqrt_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>9. Realize the actual Markov kernel</h5><p>Take the product of the identity kernel and the constant standard Gaussian kernel, then map it by G. Joint measurability proves it Markov; dirac-product and map-map give the exact pointwise pushforward. This gradient-output law is distinct from the RGO posterior and HMC transitions.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[K=(\mathrm{id}\times\gamma).\mathrm{map}(G),\quad K_s=G(s,\cdot)_\#\gamma,\quad K_s(E)=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Kernel.id; Kernel.const; Kernel.prod; Kernel.IsMarkovKernel.map; Measure.dirac_prod; Measure.map_map</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h3>Lean verification</h3><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><summary>Lean statement · full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</summary><p>Produce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the genuine quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and fixed-eta nonexpansivity. The literal Gaussian gradient evaluator G(s,z)=gradientV(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z) is jointly measurable and obeys input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta for equal eta. Produce an actual Markov kernel K(s)=stdGaussian.map(G(s,-)). This covers the exact evaluator (3.2) and only Lemma4.2 (4.4)-(4.5), with no posterior or finite-query claim.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle
    {E S : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    [MeasurableSpace S] {V : E → ℝ} {κ : ℝ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, κ⁻¹*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ≤ ‖v‖^2)
    {eta : S → ℝ} {y : S → E} (heta : Measurable eta) (hy : Measurable y)
    (hpos : ∀ s, 0 &lt; eta s) (hsmall : ∀ s, eta s ≤ 1) :
    let F := fun s x =&gt; V x + (eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
    ∃ p : S → E, Measurable p ∧
      (∀ s, p s + eta s • gradient V (p s) = y s) ∧
      (∀ s z, F s (p s) + (κ⁻¹+(eta s)⁻¹)/2*‖z-p s‖^2 ≤ F s z ∧
        (F s z ≤ F s (p s) ↔ z=p s)) ∧
      (∀ s t, eta s = eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖) ∧
      let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
      Measurable G ∧
      (∀ s t z w, eta s = eta t →
        ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖) ∧
      ∃ K : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel S E, ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel K ∧
        ∀ s, K s = (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z))</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><summary>Lean proof · full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</summary><p>Derive the real gradient estimates; Construct a uniformly damped map; Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates; Recover the exact source equation; Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness; Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta; Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator; Prove the exact input and noise constants; Realize the actual Markov kernel</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle
    {E S : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    [MeasurableSpace S] {V : E → ℝ} {κ : ℝ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, κ⁻¹*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ≤ ‖v‖^2)
    {eta : S → ℝ} {y : S → E} (heta : Measurable eta) (hy : Measurable y)
    (hpos : ∀ s, 0 &lt; eta s) (hsmall : ∀ s, eta s ≤ 1) :
    let F := fun s x =&gt; V x + (eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
    ∃ p : S → E, Measurable p ∧
      (∀ s, p s + eta s • gradient V (p s) = y s) ∧
      (∀ s z, F s (p s) + (κ⁻¹+(eta s)⁻¹)/2*‖z-p s‖^2 ≤ F s z ∧
        (F s z ≤ F s (p s) ↔ z=p s)) ∧
      (∀ s t, eta s = eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖) ∧
      let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
      Measurable G ∧
      (∀ s t z w, eta s = eta t →
        ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖) ∧
      ∃ K : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel S E, ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel K ∧
        ∀ s, K s = (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z)) := by
  let F := fun s x =&gt; V x+(eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
  obtain ⟨hLip,hMinus,hsc,hmono⟩ := actual_gradient_bounds hκ hV hH
  obtain ⟨p,hp,heq⟩ := parameterized_damped_point hMinus heta hy hpos hsmall
  have hmin := actual_quadratic_minimum hκ hV hH hpos heq
  have hnexp : ∀ s t, eta s=eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖ := by
    intro s t ht
    have hsub : y s-y t=(p s-p t)+eta s • (gradient V (p s)-gradient V (p t)) := by
      rw [← heq s,← heq t,← ht]
      module
    have hpair : ‖p s-p t‖^2 ≤ inner ℝ (y s-y t) (p s-p t) := by
      calc
        _ ≤ ‖p s-p t‖^2+eta s*inner ℝ (gradient V (p s)-gradient V (p t)) (p s-p t) :=
          le_add_of_nonneg_right (mul_nonneg (hpos s).le (hmono (p s) (p t)))
        _ = _ := by
          rw [hsub,inner_add_left,real_inner_smul_left,real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq]
    have hb := hpair.trans (real_inner_le_norm (y s-y t) (p s-p t))
    by_cases hz : ‖p s-p t‖=0
    · simpa only [hz] using norm_nonneg (y s-y t)
    · have hpositive : 0 &lt; ‖p s-p t‖ := lt_of_le_of_ne (norm_nonneg _) (Ne.symm hz)
      nlinarith
  refine ⟨p,hp,heq,hmin,hnexp,?_⟩
  let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
  have hG : Measurable G := hLip.continuous.measurable.comp
    ((hp.comp measurable_fst).add ((heta.comp measurable_fst).sqrt.smul measurable_snd))
  have hGLip : ∀ s t z w, eta s=eta t →
      ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖ := by
    intro s t z w ht
    have h := hLip.dist_le_mul (p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z) (p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w)
    simp only [NNReal.coe_one,one_mul,dist_eq_norm] at h
    have hdiff : (p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z)-(p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w) =
        (p s-p t)+Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w) := by rw [← ht]; module
    calc
      _ ≤ ‖(p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z)-(p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w)‖ := h
      _ = ‖(p s-p t)+Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w)‖ := by rw [hdiff]
      _ ≤ ‖p s-p t‖+‖Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w)‖ := norm_add_le _ _
      _ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖ := by
        rw [norm_smul,Real.norm_eq_abs,abs_of_nonneg (Real.sqrt_nonneg _)]
        exact add_le_add (hnexp s t ht) le_rfl
  let K := (Kernel.id ×ₖ Kernel.const S (stdGaussian E)).map G
  have hK : IsMarkovKernel K := Kernel.IsMarkovKernel.map _ hG
  have hKs (s : S) : K s=(stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z)) := by
    dsimp only [K]
    rw [Kernel.map_apply _ hG,Kernel.prod_apply,Kernel.id_apply,Kernel.const_apply,
      Measure.dirac_prod,Measure.map_map hG (by fun_prop)]
    rfl
  exact ⟨hG,hGLip,K,hK,hKs⟩

end AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle
end</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Source beta1 alpha=kappainv kappa&gt;=1 and0&lt;eta&lt;=1, genuine C2/global allpoint-allvector Hessian. Full-space finite real Hilbert/Borel incl0 and arbitrary measurable parameter spaces are disclosed representation extensions. Actual selector/gradient/kernel witnesses are outputs, never input certificates. Exact Gaussian gradient-output kernel is not RGO posterior or HMC K/Khat/Kbar. Old eta&lt;=1/2 construct remains preserved. Numericalprox calls sourceeta&lt;=1/(2beta) do not grant fullrange finite-query work. No standardized affine posterior law or position moment, history-integrated moment, bias4.2/GaussianMGF4.3/fullLemma4.2/Wp/higher smoothing/sampler/initialization/main/querycost/composition. TV never transfers unbounded cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h3>Source and reuse</h3><h4>Samplinglib parents called</h4><ul><li><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization-stro-17eea540ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code></a></li><li><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity-stron-258a330876"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code></a></li><li><a href="../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-smoothnessequivalences-uppe